--   rule 10: the schema documents itself
ok   every table and view carries a comment
ok   every column of every table and view carries a comment
--   and rule 11 holds inside them, because they are prose too
ok   no comment carries a dash rule 11 bans
--   and every comment still reads as the sentence somebody wrote
ok   no comment carries quoting that leaked out of the migration
ok   no comment says the same sentence twice
ok   no comment splices a full stop onto a comma
--   the same detectors, against a table nobody documented
ok   a table with nothing said about it is named
ok   spaces, a tab, and no comment at all are all named
--   and writing the comments clears it, so the check is not stuck red
ok   the table list is clean once the table is described
ok   the column list is clean once every column is described
--   the dash detector, against comments written to break the rule
ok   an emoji in a table comment is named, and so are an em dash and a wrapped path
--   and the two substitutes rule 11 names, which used to walk through
ok   a spaced hyphen and a double hyphen are both named
--   and the three shapes of garble that reached the database once already
ok   a leaked quote is named, and the two other garbled columns are not
ok   a sentence written twice is named on its own
ok   a full stop spliced onto a comma is named on its own
